C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not gather what churches say about tongues and prophecy and leave the reader to choose. It says what the author believes Paul wrote to Corinth about two gifts, it sets the verses in the order they stand, and it answers the strongest objections from named modern writers, so that a reader who disagrees knows where the disagreement lies."),
("p", "The question is narrow. In 1 Corinthians 12 to 14 Paul names tongues and prophecy among the gifts of the Spirit and spends most of three chapters on them. Three things are asked of those chapters. What are the tongues, and what is prophecy, as the chapters themselves describe them? To whom does the text say the tongues are a sign? And what does the text give as the end of the sign-program to which they belong?"),
("p", "The answers are taken from the passages. Paul says of tongues that “he that speaketh in an unknown tongue speaketh not unto men, but unto God” (1 Corinthians 14:2), and he also says that “there are, it may be, so many kinds of voices in the world, and none of them is without signification” (1 Corinthians 14:10). He quotes the prophet: “In the law it is written, With men of other tongues and other lips will I speak unto this people; and yet for all that will they not hear me, saith the Lord” (1 Corinthians 14:21). And he draws the conclusion: “Wherefore tongues are for a sign, not to them that believe, but to them that believe not” (1 Corinthians 14:22). Each sentence is read in its place, with the verses around it."),
("p", "The position is this. The tongues of 1 Corinthians 14 are languages. They are given as a sign to unbelieving Israel, on the authority of Isaiah 28:11, 12. The gifts at Corinth belong to the overlap of Acts 9 to 28, in which Israel’s Prophecy Program was still operative and the Mystery Program had begun, and they are reported as the text reports them. They end with the national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program at Acts 28. No present practice of tongues is the gift the text describes, and prophecy goes with them. The ruling rests on 1 Corinthians 14:21, 22, on Isaiah 28 and on Acts 28. It does not rest on a decision about what Paul means by “that which is perfect” in 1 Corinthians 13:10, and the volume reports that verse exactly as it is written."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the volume reads the King James Version only, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Second, each chapter opens with a claim, reads its passages in order, and closes with a short table and a list of what the volume takes from the passages, so that the reader can see what has been argued and test it. Third, where a modern writer is named, the volume says what that writer holds, in its own words, and gives the writer’s words only where it has read them in a free full text."),
("p", "The volume does not take up what its neighbours argue. The purpose of signs, their audience and the last sign recorded in Acts belong to Volume 1 of this series. Healing belongs to Volume 3, and the apostles and prophets as offices to Volume 4. The order of the assembly and the rules of 1 Corinthians 14:26 to 40 belong to Pauline Doctrine Series Volume 4, and the four scenes in which the Spirit comes in Acts to Acts Commentary Volume 2. Study 81 reports what 1 Corinthians 12 to 14 says about prayer and takes no position; Study 25 treats Mark 16, and Study 38 the Spirit’s work. This volume cites those places and does not repeat them."),
("p", "One boundary runs through the volume. Paul’s fillings and signs, and those of his companions, in Acts 9 to 28 are overlap phenomena. The volume reports them as the text reports them, does not assign them to Israel’s program and does not convert them into the Mystery. The Corinthian gifts stand in the same period and are read in the same way. What the volume argues is what the chapters say about their purpose and what the Acts record says about the end of the program they served. It makes no statement about God’s power."),
("h1", "Thesis"),
("p", "Tongues, in 1 Corinthians 12 to 14, are languages given as a sign to Israel, and Paul says so on the authority of Isaiah 28:11, 12. Prophecy in those chapters is a revealed word spoken in the assembly. Both gifts belong to the sign-program of the overlap. With the national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program at Acts 28, the sign-program associated with Israel ceases programmatically, and signs do not continue into the Mystery Program. No present practice of tongues is the gift the text describes, and prophecy, which belonged to the same program, goes with them."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in five statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("1", "The Greek word for tongue is the word for a language, and 1 Corinthians 14:10, 11 speaks of kinds of voices in the world with a meaning to each. The KJV’s italic “unknown” is a translators’ addition, and the volume reports it as such.", "Chapters 2, 4 and 7"),
    ("2", "Isaiah 28:11, 12 is spoken to the rulers of Jerusalem and to this people, and Paul quotes it in 1 Corinthians 14:21 as the written ground for tongues as a sign.", "Chapters 3 and 8"),
    ("3", "The gifts of 1 Corinthians 12 to 14 are given in the overlap, and Paul’s verbs for their end in 13:8 are reported as written, with “that which is perfect” left unidentified.", "Chapters 5, 6 and 11"),
    ("4", "Prophecy in these chapters is a word revealed to the speaker and spoken to edify, exhort and comfort. It is named in 13:8 among the things that fail, and no verse says it ended at Acts 28.", "Chapters 7, 9 and 10"),
    ("5", "With the national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program at Acts 28, the sign-program associated with Israel ceases programmatically and signs do not continue into the Mystery Program. No present practice of tongues is the biblical gift.", "Chapters 11 and 12"),
  ], [0.17, 0.61, 0.22]),
("p", "A reader may test the thesis in one move. Read 1 Corinthians 14:21, 22 aloud, then read Isaiah 28:11, 12 with the verses around it, and ask three questions: who is the people to whom the speech is a sign, what is the sign for, and what does the prophet say follows. The volume holds that the answers run through Israel and that Paul’s own conclusion in 14:22 follows from them. If a reading can be produced that keeps Paul’s quotation and gives the tongues another audience, the thesis fails at that point. The volume says plainly where its ruling rests on the text and where it rests on an inference from the text."),
]
